00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:49.940
Kicks is early.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:01:02.340
It's 1981. And it seems like it was rather popular, but I'm being completely serious when I say I had never even heard of Kicks one week before we recorded this episode.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:04.220
Oh, same. This was popular?

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:11.360
Either way, when I was playing this, I was thinking like, oh, we're gonna like, we're gonna open some, we're gonna blow some people's minds that this game exists.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.800
We're gonna be like, yeah, check out this game you've never heard of.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:21.320
It's funny that you say that because I, that was my, like before doing any research, that was my thinking as well.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:24.760
Was like, oh, this is kind of an obscure arcade game.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:25.700
It is not.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:27.220
It has many sequels.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:28.400
It has a long legacy.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:35.360
It was, again, an early arcade game published or rather released by Taito.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:40.280
So it's definitely well known by at least our audience, right?

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.480
Our audience tends to know way more than us.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.200
Yeah, 100%.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:02:13.520
And so rather than, you know, be funny or anything about Kicks and the name there, I think it's just worth mentioning that it was called Kicks because the husband-wife duo that made the game, it was named after their license plate, which was Just for Kicks, spelled J-U-S for the number four, Kicks, pronounced Just for Kicks, obviously.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:17.560
So there's no like deeper meaning to the word there or anything.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:22.900
It was just kind of like, hey, like remember that thing from my license plate that was an abbreviation?

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.800
Like what if we made that the name of the game?

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:38.680
Well, they sure did expound on that with like more lore than like some like full adventure games have as to like what Kicks is and what each, like names of different things and stuff.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.140
So like it's funny that this came from just like their license plate.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:46.420
Right, right. Because the game is very abstract and has nothing to do with driving.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:55.080
Although I guess you could say if you know, if you wanted to make it like a Tron thing that you are driving some kind of thing across a infinite space.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:04.680
But the pitch is essentially you control this diamond shaped marker on the edge of a empty rectangle or square.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:05.920
Is it square or rectangle?

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:07.060
It's a little longer, right?

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:12.360
And I guess it's both a square and rectangle because it does look square to me, but...

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.420
Okay. Yeah, it looks like a square to me now, but I wrote rectangle.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.680
So dealer's choice.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:24.680
Anyway, your job is to draw lines into the void of this parallelogram.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.360
And is that right?

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.340
Is that the right term? I think so.

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:37.880
Cool. I don't know. And you carve out your territory and that gets filled in with this cool design.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:42.980
And now you have to, you know, you get like essentially a claim percentage.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.440
So like out of that square, you just filled 5%.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:52.580
And there's a claim percentage, there's a threshold percentage and the level you're on.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.800
And so the threshold is what you have to at least meet.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:04:00.060
So if it says threshold 75%, you have to claim at least 75% of the screen.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:03.220
Sounds easy. Sounds like I'm just drawn around an empty square.

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:04.260
What's the big deal?

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:07.040
Well, there's a couple of things stopping you.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:09.160
And the biggest thing is kicks.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:28.900
This very, very abstract, kind of scary concept if it was to be visualized by man, but it is a colorful geometric figure that essentially is a bunch of lines moving very randomly throughout the open space.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:42.680
And I emphasize the randomness because it truly feels random as opposed to the Pac-Man ghosts, which as most people know, like there, it is possible to play a perfect game of Pac-Man.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:50.960
That's because the ghosts move very predictably, even though it seems random, as long as you move in the same exact movements, kicks this thing.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:05:02.320
It moves in a way that is truly unpredictable and makes it scary to be near it because at any moment, any one of its lines could go towards your line.

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:12.360
Yeah. And that is the problem is that the kicks, if it touches your line before you complete the space, you are dead.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:16.360
And it's hard to explain what kicks looks like exactly.

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:23.460
But if you're listening, I do recommend that you just look it up real quick, like a video of it just to see what this thing is.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:42.080
Like, I have to give them praise that just by like, this is this whole game is one of the like, there's one of the more simple designed like art elements like involved in this game are they're very basic.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:54.380
But like, kicks being literally just what, like five lines moving around like two different colors is does such a good job of being like chaotic and very scary.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:06:01.760
Like, it just feels like we might have mentioned this on the podcast before, but Mike, Sean and I actually went and saw the movie.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:03.620
Is it called Life?

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:05.480
Yeah. Okay.

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:08.220
The movie Life with, I believe, Jake Gyllenhaal.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:10.140
It is. Jake Gyllenhaal.

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:24.660
And yep. But this, the monster in life reminds me of kicks or vice versa, where it's just this like chaotic, so alien thing that it's just, it's funny that I'm describing this and it's just like lines on a screen.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:30.540
But like, yeah, it does feel like this scary thing where it's like, ah, this thing can get me at any second.

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:32.660
And like, it's not thinking the way I'm thinking.

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:33.920
It's just fucking moving around.

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:39.700
